+### Agents inside the sandbox (OpenCode, mini-swe-agent)
+
+Terminus-2 runs in the Harbor process and returns its tokens itself (`collect_rollout_details`). An agent that
+runs *inside* the task sandbox, such as OpenCode or mini-swe-agent, needs three things instead:
+
+1. **An adapter** that points the agent at the Gym model server: `harbor_agent_import_path`
+   `responses_api_agents.harbor_agent.custom_agents.opencode_nemo_gym:OpenCodeNemoGym` or
+   `...mini_swe_agent_nemo_gym:MiniSweAgentNemoGym`. harbor_agent passes the rollout's model URL as `api_base`.
+2. **A model server the sandbox can reach.** `harbor_model_base_url` sets the root of that URL as seen from the
+   sandbox (harbor_agent appends the rollout prefix and `/v1`). If sandboxes cannot open connections to the
+   training nodes, use the per-sandbox model tunnel below and set `harbor_model_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8000`.
+3. **Token capture at the model endpoint.** OpenCode writes no trajectory with token IDs, so the training tokens
+   can only come from Gym's capture: the run-level `token_id_capture.enabled` plus the agent's `token_id_capture`
+   (or `token_id_capture.all_agents`). NeMo RL's single-controller entrypoint turns both on with
+   `token_capture.enabled: true`. Without capture, a rollout without output items is masked, which for OpenCode
+   is every rollout.
+
+Under capture, a rollout whose calls form more than one conversation is masked (no single chain to rebuild it
+from). The OpenCode adapter therefore turns off every call besides the main loop (session title, sub-agents,
+compaction) and OpenCode's provider timeouts (a retried request is one more captured call). OpenCode does not
+send the rollout's sampling parameters (mini-swe-agent's adapter does), so set them on the model server:
+
+```yaml
+policy_model:
+  responses_api_models:
+    vllm_model:
+      sampling_overrides:
+        temperature: ${policy.generation.temperature}
+        top_p: ${policy.generation.top_p}
+        max_tokens: ${policy.generation.max_new_tokens}
+```
+
+A complete OpenCode setup on top of the training config (the `harbor_agent` block):
+
+```yaml
+harbor_agent_name: null
+harbor_agent_import_path: "responses_api_agents.harbor_agent.custom_agents.opencode_nemo_gym:OpenCodeNemoGym"
+harbor_model_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8000        # the tunnel's sandbox end
+harbor_agent_kwargs:
+  version: 1.17.11
+  opencode_install: upload                          # or binary / npm, see below
+  opencode_archive_dir: /path/to/opencode           # <dir>/<version>/<asset>
+  opencode_timeout_sec: 1740                        # below harbor_agent_max_timeout, so the verifier still runs
+  model_info:
+    max_input_tokens: ${policy.max_total_sequence_length}
+    max_output_tokens: ${policy.generation.max_new_tokens}
+harbor_environment_kwargs:
+  model_tunnel:
+    chisel_dir: /path/to/chisel
+harbor_mask_context_overflow: true                  # optional, see "Failure classes"
+```
+
+`OpenCodeNemoGym` options (`harbor_agent_kwargs`):
+
+| Option | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `version` | `1.17.11` | OpenCode release. The adapter's config keys and flags were checked against this release; another one needs `opencode_sha256` and a re-check. |
+| `opencode_install` | `binary` | `binary`: the sandbox downloads the release archive from GitHub (needs internet and python3, curl or wget). `upload`: the adapter uploads the archive from `opencode_archive_dir` (no internet, only `tar` in the image). `npm`: Harbor's own install (Node via nvm). |
+| `opencode_archive_dir` | none | For `upload`: directory with `<version>/<asset>`. The archive's sha256 is checked once per process, and again in the sandbox if `sha256sum` is there. |
+| `opencode_asset` | `opencode-linux-x64-baseline.tar.gz` | Release asset (standalone binary for glibc x86_64, no AVX2 needed). |
+| `opencode_sha256` | built in for 1.17.11 | sha256 of the archive. |
+| `opencode_timeout_sec` | none | OpenCode is ended with exit 124 after this, so its log and exit status are recorded before Harbor's agent timeout. |
+| `opencode_workdir` | none | Absolute path OpenCode runs in (created if missing); unset = the image's working directory. |
+| `opencode_config` | `{}` | Deep-merged last into OpenCode's config; unknown top-level keys make OpenCode reject the whole config. |
+| `opencode_debug` | `false` | Debug logs into `agent/opencode.txt`. |
+| `model_info` | `{}` | `max_input_tokens` / `max_output_tokens`: OpenCode's context and per-turn output limit, and the context limit for failure classes. |
+
+**Per-sandbox model tunnel** (`harbor_environment_kwargs.model_tunnel`, `NemoGymSandboxEnvironment`): for clusters
+where sandboxes cannot reach the training nodes but the node reaches the sandbox through the provider's endpoint
+(e.g. OpenSandbox's proxy or gateway, WebSockets included). At every start the environment uploads
+[chisel](https://github.com/jpillora/chisel) into the sandbox, starts `chisel server --reverse` there and
+`chisel client` in the Harbor process, so `127.0.0.1:<pod_port>` in the sandbox forwards to the model server.
+Before the trial starts it checks `GET <health_path>` through the tunnel; a tunnel that fails fails the trial in
+environment setup (failure class `infra`). The separate verifier environment gets no tunnel.
+
+| Key | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `chisel_dir` | required | Directory with `chisel_<chisel_version>_linux_{amd64,arm64}` (released binaries, gunzipped; not shipped). |
+| `chisel_version` | `1.12.0` | |
+| `target` | the model server | `host:port` the node-side client dials; harbor_agent fills it per run. |
+| `pod_port` / `server_port` | `8000` / `8765` | Loopback port in the sandbox / port of the chisel server in the sandbox. |
+| `health_path` | `/health` | Checked from inside the sandbox; `null` skips it. |
+| `connect_timeout_s` | `60` | Bound on the client's first connection. |
+| `tls_skip_verify` | `false` | Skip certificate checks on the endpoint's TLS. |
+| `max_retry_count` | `10` | Client reconnects after a drop. |
+
+The image needs only `uname`, `setsid` or `nohup`, and `bash` for the health check.
+
+For debugging, Gym's model-call capture (`model_call_capture_dir` in the global config) writes every request and
+response at the model endpoint per rollout; it shows per-turn lengths, context overflows and 4xx bodies, which
+an in-sandbox agent's own logs often do not.
+
